course: dictionary
lesson: 876
title: Binary: counting with only 0 and 1
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Our normal counting uses ten digits, 0 to 9, so each place is worth ten times the one to its right: ones, tens, hundreds. Binary uses only two digits, 0 and 1. «Binary has no digit 2, so after 0111 comes 1000, just as after 0999 comes 1000 in normal numbers.» When a place holds a 1 and needs one more, it rolls over to 0 and carries a 1 to the left.
explain: In binary, each place is worth double the one to its right. Reading from the right, the places are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. «Binary 1011 has place values 8, 4, 2, 1, so it is 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.» You add up the place values where there is a 1, and ignore the places with a 0.
explain: Binary matters because it is how computers work. «Computers store everything, every photo, song and game, as ones and zeros, called binary, like switches that are on or off.» A 1 means on and a 0 means off. Every picture on a screen is built from these switches.
fact: Binary is a way of writing numbers using only the digits 0 and 1.
fact: Computers use binary because each switch in a computer has only two states: on or off.
fact: Each single 0 or 1 is called a bit.
fact: In binary, the place values from the right are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, each worth double the one before.
fact: A binary star is a pair of stars that go round each other.
exercise: What number comes after 0111 in binary? | *1000 | 1111 | 0112 | 0200
exercise: Binary 1011 has place values 8, 4, 2, 1. What is its value in our normal numbers? | 9 | *11 | 1001 | 101
exercise: In binary, how much is each place worth compared with the place to its right? | Half as much | *Double | Ten times as much | The same
test: Binary 0101 has place values 8, 4, 2, 1. What is its value in normal numbers? | 6 | *5 | 101 | 4
test: Computers store every photo, song and game as what? | *Ones and zeros | Tens and hundreds | Words and pictures | Letters from A to Z
exam: Binary 1110 has place values 8, 4, 2, 1. What is its value in normal numbers? | 1000 | 7 | *14 | 11
